MAIN_SHEET
Index	SS / Module	Test Case Name	Feature	Test Description	Test Steps / Procedure	Impacted Registers	Validation / Acceptance Criteria	Speed	Mode	Remarks
1	GPIO	gpio_reg_wr_rd_test	Register Read/Write Validation	This test validates the reset default values and read-write functionality of three GPIO registers: gp0_gpio_8, gp0_gpio_9, and gp0_gpio_10. In the first phase, each register is read after reset and its value (with the hardware-driven data_in bit masked out) is compared against the expected default value to confirm correct reset behavior. In the second phase, six distinct data patterns are written to the writable fields of each register and read back. The read-back value is verified against an expected value that accounts for read-only bits retaining their default state and write-only bits not being readable. The test reports pass if all default value checks and all write-read verifications succeed across all three registers and all six patterns, and reports fail otherwise.	1. Initialize the test environment and ensure the GPIO IP block is in its reset state.
2. Read each of the three GPIO registers (gp0_gpio_8, gp0_gpio_9, gp0_gpio_10) and mask out the hardware-driven pin-level input bit (bit 0).
3. Compare each masked read value against the expected reset default value for that register. Record any mismatches as default-value failures.
4. For each of six test data patterns (all-ones, 0xAAAAAAAA, 0x55555555, 0xF5F5F5F5, 0xA5A5A5A5, 0xFFFF0000), write the pattern (masked to writable bits only) to all three GPIO registers.
5. Read back each register after writing, applying the read mask to isolate readable bits.
6. Compute the expected read-back value accounting for writable bits receiving the written pattern and read-only bits retaining their default values.
7. Compare the actual read-back value against the computed expected value. Record any mismatches as write-read failures.
8. Repeat steps 4 through 7 for all six data patterns.
9. Evaluate the final result: if any default-value or write-read failure was recorded, report FAIL; otherwise report PASS.	gp0_gpio_8; gp0_gpio_9; gp0_gpio_10	1. All three GPIO registers (gp0_gpio_8, gp0_gpio_9, gp0_gpio_10) must return their expected reset default values after masking out the hardware-driven input bit.
2. For each of the six test data patterns, the read-back value from each register must match the expected value computed from the write mask, read mask, and default values.
3. Read-only fields must retain their default values regardless of the written pattern.
4. Write-only fields must accept writes without affecting readable bit positions.
5. The test must report PASS (exit code 0) when all checks succeed and FAIL (exit code 1) when any check fails.	NA	NA	The test masks out bit 0 (data_in field) during default value checks because this read-only field reflects the external GPIO pin level, which may not match the register specification default. A comment in the source notes that the data_in value becomes 1 automatically if no value is forced, and forcing zero causes the level_sel bit to go high, leading to mismatches. The soft reset check function is present in the source but is disabled (compiled out). Six registers in the skip arrays are flagged to be skipped during write-read checks, and twelve are skipped during reset-value checks, to handle special register types (e.g., volatile read-write registers).

METADATA_SHEET
Index	SS / Module	Test Case Name	Feature	Meta Test Description	Meta Test Steps / Procedure	Meta Impacted Registers	Test Description	Test Steps / Procedure	Impacted Registers	Validation / Acceptance Criteria	Speed	Mode	Remarks
1	GPIO	gpio_reg_wr_rd_test	Register Read/Write Validation	This testcase validates the default (reset) values and read-write accessibility of GPIO GP0 registers (MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8, MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_9, MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_10) at base address 0xA001A000 with offsets 0x0, 0x4, and 0x8 respectively. The test is structured in two phases: (1) chk_rst_val() iterates over addr_array[0..CNT-1], skips entries flagged in skip_rst_array or where read_mask_array is 0x00000000, reads each register via read_reg(addr), masks the read value with 0xfffffffe (masking out bit 0 the data_in RO field whose value depends on external pin state), and compares against default_value_array[i]. Mismatches increment def_fail_cnt. (2) chk_rd_wr() iterates over 6 test data patterns {0xffffffff, 0xaaaaaaaa, 0x55555555, 0xf5f5f5f5, 0xA5A5A5A5, 0xffff0000}. For each pattern, it writes (data_wr & write_mask_array[i]) to each register (skipping entries flagged in skip_array or where write_mask_array is 0x00000000), then reads back via read_reg(addr) & read_mask_array[i]. The expected value is computed as ((data_wr & read_mask_array[i] & write_mask_array[i]) | (wr_n & read_mask_array[i] & default_value_array[i])) where wr_n = (write_mask_array[i] ^ 0xffffffff). This accounts for read-only bits retaining their default values. Mismatches increment wr_fail_cnt. Finally, test_case() checks if def_fail_cnt > 0 or wr_fail_cnt > 0 and calls finish(1) for failure or finish(0) for pass. The soft_reset_chk() function exists but is disabled inside #ifdef 0. The test includes gpio/gpio_def.h and gpio/gpio_offset.h headers for macro definitions.	1. Entry: test_case() is called.
2. Phase 1 Default Value Check (chk_rst_val()):
   a. Loop i = 0 to CNT-1 (49 iterations over addr_array).
   b. addr = addr_array[i] (MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8, MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_9, MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_10, ...).
   c. If skip_rst_array[i] == 1, skip this register (continue).
   d. If read_mask_array[i] == 0x00000000, skip (register not readable).
   e. data_rd = read_reg(addr).
   f. data = (data_rd & 0xfffffffe) mask out bit 0 (data_in pin-level RO field).
   g. Compare data against default_value_array[i].
   h. If mismatch: def_fail_cnt++ and print failure with address, expected, and actual values.
   i. If match: print pass (under DEBUG_DISPLAY).
3. Phase 2 Write and Read Check (chk_rd_wr()):
   a. Define chk_val[6] = {0xffffffff, 0xaaaaaaaa, 0x55555555, 0xf5f5f5f5, 0xA5A5A5A5, 0xffff0000}.
   b. Outer loop j = 0 to 5 (iterate over 6 test patterns).
   c. data_wr = chk_val[j].
   d. Write sub-loop: for i = 0 to CNT-1: addr = addr_array[i]; If skip_array[i] == 1, skip; If write_mask_array[i] == 0x00000000, skip (not writable); write_reg(addr, (data_wr & write_mask_array[i])).
   e. Read-back sub-loop: for i = 0 to CNT-1: addr = addr_array[i]; If skip_array[i] == 1, skip; If write_mask_array[i] == 0x00000000, skip; If read_mask_array[i] == 0x00000000, skip; data_rd = read_reg(addr) & read_mask_array[i]; wr_n = (write_mask_array[i] ^ 0xffffffff); exp_val = ((data_wr & read_mask_array[i] & write_mask_array[i]) | (wr_n & read_mask_array[i] & default_value_array[i])); Compare data_rd against exp_val; If mismatch: wr_fail_cnt++ and print failure; If match: print pass (under DEBUG_DISPLAY).
4. Final verdict: if (def_fail_cnt > 0 || wr_fail_cnt > 0) then finish(1) [FAIL], else finish(0) [PASS].	MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8; MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_9; MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_10	This test validates the reset default values and read-write functionality of three GPIO registers: gp0_gpio_8, gp0_gpio_9, and gp0_gpio_10. In the first phase, each register is read after reset and its value (with the hardware-driven data_in bit masked out) is compared against the expected default value to confirm correct reset behavior. In the second phase, six distinct data patterns are written to the writable fields of each register and read back. The read-back value is verified against an expected value that accounts for read-only bits retaining their default state and write-only bits not being readable. The test reports pass if all default value checks and all write-read verifications succeed across all three registers and all six patterns, and reports fail otherwise.	1. Initialize the test environment and ensure the GPIO IP block is in its reset state.
2. Read each of the three GPIO registers (gp0_gpio_8, gp0_gpio_9, gp0_gpio_10) and mask out the hardware-driven pin-level input bit (bit 0).
3. Compare each masked read value against the expected reset default value for that register. Record any mismatches as default-value failures.
4. For each of six test data patterns (all-ones, 0xAAAAAAAA, 0x55555555, 0xF5F5F5F5, 0xA5A5A5A5, 0xFFFF0000), write the pattern (masked to writable bits only) to all three GPIO registers.
5. Read back each register after writing, applying the read mask to isolate readable bits.
6. Compute the expected read-back value accounting for writable bits receiving the written pattern and read-only bits retaining their default values.
7. Compare the actual read-back value against the computed expected value. Record any mismatches as write-read failures.
8. Repeat steps 4 through 7 for all six data patterns.
9. Evaluate the final result: if any default-value or write-read failure was recorded, report FAIL; otherwise report PASS.	gp0_gpio_8; gp0_gpio_9; gp0_gpio_10	1. All three GPIO registers (gp0_gpio_8, gp0_gpio_9, gp0_gpio_10) must return their expected reset default values after masking out the hardware-driven input bit.
2. For each of the six test data patterns, the read-back value from each register must match the expected value computed from the write mask, read mask, and default values.
3. Read-only fields must retain their default values regardless of the written pattern.
4. Write-only fields must accept writes without affecting readable bit positions.
5. The test must report PASS (exit code 0) when all checks succeed and FAIL (exit code 1) when any check fails.	NA	NA	The test masks out bit 0 (data_in field) during default value checks because this read-only field reflects the external GPIO pin level, which may not match the register specification default. A comment in the source notes that the data_in value becomes 1 automatically if no value is forced, and forcing zero causes the level_sel bit to go high, leading to mismatches. The soft reset check function is present in the source but is disabled (compiled out). Six registers in the skip arrays are flagged to be skipped during write-read checks, and twelve are skipped during reset-value checks, to handle special register types (e.g., volatile read-write registers).